{# How a generated page links THE STYLESHEET — one link, to whichever bundle
   the CALLER asked for. It lives beside the bundle it loads;
   js/js.loader.html.j2 is its twin, and both shells include the pair:

     {% include "css/css.loader.html.j2" %}
     {% include "js/js.loader.html.j2" %}

   `asset_bundles` is "local" or "cdn". A report takes it from
   `--asset-bundles`, which defaults to "cdn"; a showcase is always local. The
   default goes to the portable half because the two are not symmetric — "cdn"
   renders everywhere including where the file was written, "local" only there.

   `local_href` is the relative path from WHERE THE FILE IS WRITTEN back to the
   skill — a showcase three folders deep gets `../../..`, a report in docs/ gets
   whatever reaches the skill from there. A destination with no relative path at
   all (a different Windows drive) cannot take "local", and
   check_asset_bundles() refuses that combination before the build starts rather
   than emitting an href that cannot resolve.

   ONE LINK, NO FALLBACK. Until 12.0.0 every page linked local-first with the
   CDN armed on `onerror`, because the page could not know where it had been
   written. That cost a 404 on every page that had left the tree — and a
   stylesheet that 404s counts as RESOLVED, so the browser painted the whole
   document unstyled before the fallback was even armed. The flash was the
   fallback. Choosing at build time removes both, and the page that says "local"
   is the page that meant it. #}
{% if asset_bundles == "local" %}
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ local_href }}/css/bundle.css">
{% else %}
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ cdn_href }}/css/bundle.css">
{% endif %}
